\section{Name Computations Without Erasing Meaning}

A functional intermediate language is useful when its grammar makes the compiler's next question easy to ask. For evaluating or optimizing a strict program, a good question is which computation runs next. Erik Oosting's \emph{A fast instruction language for functional programs} uses administrative normal form (ANF) as input and distinguishes atomic expressions from computations. In a typical strict ANF, operands of calls and primitives are atomic, while computations occur in right-hand sides of bindings or in tail position. A bound variable is atomic because it names an already evaluated value; reading a lazy variable that may force a thunk does not satisfy the same operational contract. Thus even this apparently syntactic distinction depends on the IR semantics.

For example, a left-to-right strict compiler can turn \texttt{f (g x) (h y)} into a sequence that binds \texttt{a = g x}, then \texttt{b = h y}, then calls \texttt{f a b}, assuming that \texttt{f} is a value and the notation denotes a two-argument call. If \texttt{g} raises an exception, \texttt{h} does not run. Normalizing a conditional likewise puts bindings from each arm \emph{inside} that arm. A pass checker can enforce atomic operands and lexical scope, but checking those properties does not establish the evaluation-order invariant; the transformation must preserve sequencing by construction.

\section{From Evaluation Contexts to Join Points}

An ANF binding says what to do with one result; continuation-passing style (CPS) can name that future explicitly. A call in CPS has a continuation operand in addition to its ordinary operands. A known, nonescaping continuation called only in tail position need not be a general closure: it can be a local join point whose parameters resemble SSA block parameters. By contrast, a continuation stored in a heap object cannot generally be compiled as a local branch. Lowering depends on how the label is used, not on whether the syntax calls it a continuation.

Consider a conditional followed by \texttt{use v}, where the conditional chooses between \texttt{inc x} and \texttt{dec x}. Build a join \texttt{done(v)} containing the call to \texttt{use}; both branches transfer to it with their own value. This avoids copying the post-conditional code. A control-flow checker should verify that incoming actuals match the block parameter list and that any external value used by the join dominates it. If a branch instead invokes an exception handler, it must not pass an invented value to \texttt{done}. Test with a successful branch and a failing branch and require exactly one entry to \texttt{done} across the two runs.

\section{Worked Example: Make Graph Operations Visible}

P\'eter D\'avid Podlovics, Csaba Hruska, and Andor P\'enzes, in \emph{A modern look at GRIN, an optimizing functional language back end}, describe making graph operations explicit with \texttt{store}, \texttt{fetch}, and \texttt{update}. Their whole-program setting allows a generated \texttt{eval} function to be analyzed like other code. In a simplified GRIN-like example, store a node \texttt{SuspAdd(20,1)} and pass its address \texttt{p} to \texttt{eval}. If heap analysis gives that node tag as the only possibility on entry, specialize the dispatch to that case. A generic evaluator still needs a separate path for a node that is already a value. Calling \texttt{eval p} twice checks this distinction: the first call evaluates and updates; the second must observe the new tag, not reuse the pre-update abstract fact. A specialization may remove dispatch, but it cannot discard a required update just because the address is locally known.

These representations keep different facts visible. ANF gives names to intermediate results, CPS exposes control transfers, and a heap-oriented IR exposes allocation and demand. None is an obligation to lower everything immediately to loads and jumps. \emph{The Modern Compiler Dossier}, Chapter~15, gives the complementary argument for preserving semantic operators until choosing a lowering. In a pipeline with both levels, a pass should declare which facts its target grammar now encodes, which facts it preserves only as metadata, and which it deliberately forgets.

