\section{Forward a Heap Fetch Only While Its Tag Fact Is Current}

In a straight-line graph IR, a known node value can justify more than replacing evaluator dispatch: it can sometimes eliminate the fetch itself. Assume a sequential, nonvolatile heap with valid addresses and no observable fetch operation other than its result. The input is one block of allocations, strong updates, fetches, and calls; allocation sites in this local analysis denote fresh dynamic addresses. Assume a conservative test for whether two address operands may alias. The pass tracks already evaluated node values and replaces a fetch only when its address has a current fact. It preserves all stores and their order.

\begin{verbatim}
forward(block):
  known = empty map
  for instruction in block:
    case instruction of
      store p, value: keep; known[p] = value
      update p, value:
        # A write can invalidate even a different name.
        erase known[q] for each q may_alias p
        keep; known[p] = value
      fetch p:
        if p in known: replace fetch by known[p]
        else: keep
      call f:
        # Unknown code can reach any tracked node.
        if f is not proved heap-read-only: clear known
        keep
\end{verbatim}

Allocate \(p\) with \verb|Value(2)| and \(q\) with \verb|Value(9)|. After \verb|update p Value(4)|, fetches of \(p\) and \(q\) can become \verb|Value(4)| and \verb|Value(9)| if their allocations are known disjoint. If a separate operand \texttt{r} aliases \(p\) and an update through it writes \verb|Value(7)|, a later fetch through \(p\) must return \verb|Value(7)|, not the earlier \verb|Value(4)|. The pass does not track must-alias facts, so it simply keeps that fetch. A call that can force a thunk is not heap-read-only merely because the source language is pure: forcing may change the node at its address.

At every program point each table entry names the exact node value stored at its address on any execution reaching that point. Clearing the table at a join is conservative; retaining facts there needs agreement on every incoming path and availability of the substituted value. This simple implementation scans the table at each update. Memory versions and indexed alias sets can narrow that cost, but they do not in themselves establish the alias facts.
